)>>endobj21071 0 obj<</BBox 21072 0 R/Height 121.17/O/Layout/Width 249.19>>endobj21072 0 obj[96.583 481.115 345.773 602.285]endobj21073 0 obj<</A 21074 0 R/Alt(A tree-based signature scheme uses a binary tree structure where each node contains a public key and its corresponding signature. At the root, the public key p k epsilon is signed with sigma epsilon, followed by concatenations of keys and signatures along\ the branches. At the leaves, messages m equal to 1 0 1 and m equal to 1 1 1 are signed, each with a complete path of public keys and signatures leading from the root to the leaf.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 8020 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21074 0 obj<</BBox 21075 0 R/Height 171.706/O/Layout/Width 186.487>>endobj21075 0 obj[133.598 427.055 320.085 598.761]endobj21076 0 obj<</A 21077 0 R/Alt(In chain-based signatures, each message m sub i is signed with signature sigma sub i and linked to the next public key p k sub i plus 1. Initially, the first two messages m 1 and m 2 are signed and linked to keys p k 2 and p k 3. After signing message m 3\, it is linked with signature sigma sub 3 and public key p k 4, forming a sequential chain of signatures and keys.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 7987 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21077 0 obj<</BBox 21078 0 R/Height 160.169/O/Layout/Width 245.197>>endobj21078 0 obj[98.8411 430.092 344.038 590.26]endobj21079 0 obj<</A 21080 0 R/Alt(The Lamport signature scheme for message m equals 0 1 1 is displayed. \r\n\r\nThe secret key matrix s k has 2 rows and 3 columns with entries x 1 0, x 2 0, x 3 0 in the top row and x 1 1, x 2 1, x 3 1 in the bottom row. For message 0 1 1, the signature si\gma consists of x 1 0, x 2 1, and x 3 1. The verification uses the public key matrix p k with 2 rows and 3 columns, containing y 1 0, y 2 0, y 3 0 in the top row and y 1 1, y 2 1, y 3 1 in the bottom row. Hash values of the signature components H of x 1, \H of x 2, and H of x 3 are checked against y 1 0, y 2 1, and y 3 1, respectively, for equality.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 7923 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21080 0 obj<</BBox 21081 0 R/Height 117.748/O/Layout/Width 238.86>>endobj21081 0 obj[101.936 124.696 340.796 242.444]endobj21082 0 obj<</A 21083 0 R/Alt(Execution of the Schnorr identification scheme involves a prover with input x and a verifier with group parameters G, q, generator g, and public key y. \r\n\r\nThe prover selects k from Z sub q and computes I as g to the power of k, then sends I to the ve\rifier. The verifier selects a random challenge 'r' from Z sub q and sends it to the prover. The prover computes s as r times x plus k modulo q and sends s back. The verifier checks whether g to the power of s times y to the power of negative r equals I.␀\)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 7540 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21083 0 obj<</BBox 21084 0 R/Height 110.507/O/Layout/Width 279.444>>endobj21084 0 obj[81.7461 83.4799 361.19 193.987]endobj21085 0 obj<</A 21086 0 R/Alt(A three-round identification scheme is illustrated between a prover with secret key s k and a verifier with public key p k. \r\n\r\nThe prover runs a function P 1 on s k to generate a value I and state s t, then sends I to the verifier. The verifier gener\ates a random challenge r from a distribution omega sub p k and sends r to the prover. The prover computes a response s using function P 2 on s k, s t, and r, and sends s back. The verifier checks whether the verification function V on p k, r, and s equal\s I.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 7493 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21086 0 obj<</BBox 21087 0 R/Height 101.722/O/Layout/Width 212.118>>endobj21087 0 obj[115.409 158.713 327.527 260.435]endobj21088 0 obj<</A 21089 0 R/Alt(A rectangular structure with four tuples represents public key operations and encryptions. \r\n\r\nThe top left tuple is \(p k, Encaps sub p k superscript 1 of 1 superscript n, Enc sub k prime of m 0\). An arrow labeled by security of Pi points downward t\o the bottom left tuple \(p k, Encaps sub p k superscript 1 of 1 superscript n, Enc sub k prime prime of m 0\). An arrow labeled by security of Pi prime connects this bottom left tuple to the bottom right tuple \(p k, Encaps sub p k superscript 1 of 1 sup\erscript n, Enc sub k prime prime of m 1\). An upward arrow labeled by security of Pi connects this to the top right tuple \(p k, Encaps sub p k superscript 1 of 1 superscript n, Enc sub k prime of m 1\). A dashed arrow labeled by transitivity connects th\e top left to the top right tuple.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 6550 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21089 0 obj<</BBox 21090 0 R/Height 75.4914/O/Layout/Width 340.028>>endobj21090 0 obj[56.9928 513.655 397.02 589.146]endobj21091 0 obj<</A 21092 0 R/Alt(A key encapsulation mechanism, labeled Encaps, takes a public key p k and outputs a ciphertext c and a key k. This key k is input into a private-key encryption function Enc prime, which encrypts a message m to produce ciphertext c prime.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 6498 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21092 0 obj<</BBox 21093 0 R/Height 110.437/O/Layout/Width 148.144>>endobj21093 0 obj[144.066 226.733 292.21 337.17]endobj21094 0 obj<</A 21095 0 R/Alt(A hybrid encryption system begins with a public key input p k and a session key k input into a public-key encryption function Enc. The output is ciphertext c. The same key k is also used in a private-key encryption function Enc prime, which takes a messag\e m and produces ciphertext c prime.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 6489 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21095 0 obj<</BBox 21096 0 R/Height 110.916/O/Layout/Width 148.144>>endobj21096 0 obj[144.066 308.003 292.21 418.919]endobj21097 0 obj<</A 21098 0 R/Alt(A table compares cryptographic primitives in private-key and public-key settings. For secrecy, the private-key setting uses private-key encryption, while the public-key setting uses public-key encryption. For integrity, the private-key setting uses messag\e authentication codes, and the public-key setting uses digital signature schemes.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 6256 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21098 0 obj<</BBox 21099 0 R/Height 43.437/O/Layout/Width 321.915>>endobj21099 0 obj[63.1121 558.661 385.027 602.098]endobj21100 0 obj<</A 21101 0 R/Alt(In the Diffie-Hellman key exchange protocol, Alice chooses a value x from Z sub q and computes h sub A as g to the power of x. Bob chooses a value y from Z sub q and computes h sub B as g to the power of y. Alice sends h sub A, Bob sends h sub B. Alice co\mputes the shared key k A as h sub B to the power of x, and Bob computes it as h sub A to the power of y. Both derive the same shared key.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 6200 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21101 0 obj<</BBox 21102 0 R/Height 164.028/O/Layout/Width 200.576>>endobj21102 0 obj[124.428 437.812 325.003 601.84]endobj21103 0 obj<</A 21104 0 R/Alt(A key distribution protocol involves three parties: Alice, Bob, and a key distribution center. Alice sends a message to the center stating she wants to talk to Bob. The center responds by sending a session key k encrypted under both Alices and Bobs resp\ective keys, Enc sub k A of k and Enc sub k B of k. Alice forwards the encrypted key to Bob. Bob receives the encrypted key under his own key.\r\n\r\n␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 6153 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21104 0 obj<</BBox 21105 0 R/Height 143.481/O/Layout/Width 219.679>>endobj21105 0 obj[111.527 452.654 331.206 596.135]endobj21106 0 obj<</A 21107 0 R/Alt(An elliptic curve defined by the equation y squared equals x cubed minus x plus 1 is graphed. Three points P 1, P 2, and P 3 are marked on the curve. A diagonal line intersects all three points. The reflection of P 3 over the x-axis is labeled as negative\ P 3, representing the elliptic curve group addition of P 1 and P 2.␀)/K 0/P 20969 0 R/Pg 5557 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21107 0 obj<</BBox 21108 0 R/Height 156.812/O/Layout/Width 174.628>>endobj21108 0 obj[134.112 441.363 308.74 598.175]endobj21109 0 obj<</A 21110 0 R/Alt(A hierarchical flowchart represents a top-down structure in private-key cryptography. Pseudorandom permutations are central, leading upward to private-key encryption and message authentication codes, which correspond to Chapters 3, 4, and 5. Below, pseudo\random permutations connect to block ciphers in Chapter 7, and to pseudorandom generators and one-way functions in Chapter 8. One-way functions depend on number-theoretic problems such as R S A, factoring, and discrete logarithm, which are introduced in t\he current chapter.␀)/K 0/P 21111 0 R/Pg 5037 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj21110 0 obj<</BBox 21130 0 R/Height 214.57/O/Layout/Width 242.11>>endobj21111 0 obj<</K[21112 0 R 21113 0 R 21114 0 R 21115 0 R 21116 0 R 21117 0 R 21109 0 R]/P 20969 0 R/S/Document>>endobj21112 0 obj<</K 21128 0 R/P 21111 0 R/S/Article>>endobj21113 0 obj<</K 21126 0 R/P 21111 0 R/S/Article>>endobj21114 0 obj<</K 21124 0 R/P 21111 0 R/S/Article>>endobj21115 0 obj<</K 21122 0 R/P 21111 0 R/S/Article>>endobj21116 0 obj<</K 21120 0 R/P 21111 0 R/S/Article>>endobj21117 0 obj<</K 21118 0 R/P 21111 0 R/S/Article>>endobj21118 0 obj<</A 21119 0 R/K 1066/P 21117 0 R/Pg 9149 0 R/S/Artifact>>endobj21119 0 obj<</BBox[85.8624 295.894 356.342 367.414]/O/Layout/Placement/Block>>endobj21120 0 obj<</A 21121 0 R/K 1066/P 21116 0 R/Pg 4985 0 R/S/Artifact>>endobj21121 0 obj<</BBox[85.8624 295.894 356.342 367.414]/O/Layout/Placement/Block>>endobj21122 0 obj<</A 21123 0 R/K 1066/P 21115 0 R/Pg 1295 0 R/S/Artifact>>endobj21123 0 obj<</BBox[85.8624 295.894 356.342 367.414]/O/Layout/Placement/Block>>endobj21124 0 obj<</A 21125 0 R/K 1066/P 21114 0 R/Pg 840 0 R/S/Artifact>>endobj21125 0 obj<</BBox[85.8624 295.894 356.342 367.414]/O/Layout/Placement/Block>>endobj21126 0 obj<</A 21127 0 R/K 1066/P 21113 0 R/Pg 679 0 R/S/Artifact>>endobj21127 0 obj<</BBox[85.8624 295.894 356.342 367.414]/O/Layout/Placement/Block>>endobj21128 0 obj<</A 21129 0 R/K 1066/P 21112 0 R/Pg 56 0 R/S/Artifact>>endo